`getApprovalWorkflowRulesForPolicy(rulesCollection, policyID)` runs on every render, iterating the policy-filtered `RULE` collection and building an index map per rule. Its result is only consumed by `convertApprovalWorkflowRulesToWorkflows` on the `MULTIPLE_APPROVERS` beta branch — `convertPolicyEmployeesToApprovalWorkflows` (the default path when the beta is off) never reads `rules`, so this work is discarded on the common path.

Compute the rules only when the beta is enabled, so the expensive call is skipped otherwise:

```ts
const isMultipleApprovers = isBetaEnabled(CONST.BETAS.MULTIPLE_APPROVERS);

if (!isMultipleApprovers) {
    return convertPolicyEmployeesToApprovalWorkflows({policy, personalDetails: personalDetails ?? {}, localeCompare, firstApprover, currentUserLogin});
}

return convertApprovalWorkflowRulesToWorkflows({
    policy,
    personalDetails: personalDetails ?? {},
    localeCompare,
    firstApprover,
    currentUserLogin,
    rules: getApprovalWorkflowRulesForPolicy(rulesCollection, policyID),
});
```

`getDisplayTransactionWithoutInvalidCommuterExclusion` is called unconditionally by `getTransactionReportName` (the LHN/report-name hot path) and `MoneyRequestView` for every transaction. For the common case (a transaction with no applied commuter exclusion), this line calls `getDistanceRateForDisplay`, which can iterate over the entire `allPolicies` collection resolving a rate, and only *afterwards* does line 250 short-circuit with the cheap `!hasAppliedCommuterExclusion(transaction)` / `typeof fullDistance !== 'number'` checks. The expensive rate lookup runs before the cheap guard that would discard it.

Move the cheap guards before the expensive rate resolution so non-commuter and non-distance transactions return early without scanning policies:

```ts
const customUnit = transaction.comment?.customUnit;
const fullDistance = customUnit?.quantity;
const distanceUnit = customUnit?.distanceUnit;
if (!hasAppliedCommuterExclusion(transaction) || typeof fullDistance !== 'number') {
    return transaction;
}

const mileageRate = getDistanceRateForDisplay({customUnitRateID: customUnit?.customUnitRateID, policy, policies});
const rate = mileageRate?.rate;
const unit = distanceUnit ?? mileageRate?.unit;
if (!unit || !rate) {
    return transaction;
}
```

The `selectedItems` spread (`[...selectedReports, ...Object.values(selectedTransactions)]`) was hoisted above the `if (areAllMatchingItemsSelected)` branch, so it now allocates a new array on every memo recomputation even in the `areAllMatchingItemsSelected` path, which returns without ever using `selectedItems`. Likewise `getFilterFromQuery(...)` on the line above now runs even in the else branch that never reads it. Each was previously scoped to the branch that needs it, avoiding the wasted work.

Move each computation back inside the branch that consumes it so the cheap `areAllMatchingItemsSelected` check short-circuits first:

```ts
const doAllSelectedItemsBelongToCADPolicies = useMemo(() => {
    if (areAllMatchingItemsSelected) {
        const policyIDFilter = getFilterFromQuery(queryJSON, CONST.SEARCH.SYNTAX_FILTER_KEYS.POLICY_ID);
        if (!policyIDFilter.value?.length || policyIDFilter.isNegated) {
            return false;
        }
        return policyIDFilter.value.every((policyID) => policies?.[`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY}${policyID}`]?.outputCurrency === CONST.CURRENCY.CAD);
    }

    const selectedItems = [...selectedReports, ...Object.values(selectedTransactions)];
    if (selectedItems.length === 0) {
        return false;
    }
    // ...
}, [...]);
```

Same issue as in `createTodosReportsAndTransactions`: `didCurrentUserPlaceHoldOnReportExpense` (which scans all report actions and does a `.find` over transactions per action) is computed unconditionally for every report, even though `currentUserPlacedHold` is only consulted when `allExpensesHeld` is true (`!allExpensesHeld || currentUserPlacedHold`). The cheap `hasOnlyHeldExpenses(reportTransactions)` result is already available for `allExpensesHeld`, so short-circuit the expensive derivation behind it.

```ts
const allExpensesHeld = hasOnlyHeldExpenses(reportTransactions);
const context: TodoBucketContext = {
    // ...
    allExpensesHeld,
    currentUserPlacedHold:
        allExpensesHeld &&
        didCurrentUserPlaceHoldOnReportExpense(
            allReportActions?.[`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_ACTIONS}${report.reportID}`],
            reportTransactions,
            currentUserAccountID,
        ),
    // ...
};
```

`didCurrentUserPlaceHoldOnReportExpense` iterates every report action and, for each money-request action, does a `.find` over the transactions plus a `getReportAction` lookup, so it is meaningfully more expensive than the surrounding per-report work. Here it runs unconditionally for every expense report in the loop, but the consumers (`APPROVE`/`PAY` predicates) only read `currentUserPlacedHold` when `allExpensesHeld` is true (`!allExpensesHeld || currentUserPlacedHold`). The cheap `hasOnlyHeldExpenses(reportTransactions)` guard is already computed one line above for `allExpensesHeld`, so this expensive call can be short-circuited for the large majority of reports that are not all-held.

Gate the expensive call behind the held check:

```ts
const allExpensesHeld = hasOnlyHeldExpenses(reportTransactions);
const context: TodoBucketContext = {
    // ...
    allExpensesHeld,
    currentUserPlacedHold:
        allExpensesHeld &&
        didCurrentUserPlaceHoldOnReportExpense(
            allReportActions?.[`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT_ACTIONS}${report.reportID}`],
            reportTransactions,
            currentUserAccountID,
        ),
    // ...
};
```

The new `getOwnedPaidPolicies(policies, currentUserAccountID)` call iterates the entire `POLICY` collection (`Object.values(...).filter(...)`, O(n)) and is placed before three trivial O(1) guards that follow it in this `useMemo`: `isLoadingOnyxValue(billingFundIDResult)`, `doesUserHavePaymentCardAdded(billingFundID)`, and `readinessState !== READINESS_STATE.READY`. Whenever any of those cheap checks would short-circuit to `false`, the expensive collection scan runs needlessly on every recompute.

Move the cheap checks ahead of the expensive iteration so the whole policy collection is only scanned once the trivial preconditions pass:

```ts
if (!isUserOnFreeTrial(firstDayFreeTrial, lastDayFreeTrial)) {
    return false;
}
if (isLoadingOnyxValue(billingFundIDResult)) {
    return false;
}
if (doesUserHavePaymentCardAdded(billingFundID)) {
    return false;
}
if (readinessState !== READINESS_STATE.READY) {
    return false;
}
if (!getOwnedPaidPolicies(policies, currentUserAccountID).length) {
    return false;
}
```

`willReportBecomeOneTransactionReportAfterMerge(...)` is the expensive computation in this branch — it merges the entire Search snapshot (`currentSearchResults?.data`) and the report's transaction collection into a `Map` and iterates over all of it. It runs unconditionally here, but the guarded `if` below (`if (!willTargetReportBeOneTransactionReport && (isTargetThreadTopmost || isTargetReportUnderneath))`) also requires the much cheaper `isTargetThreadTopmost`/`isTargetReportUnderneath` checks (which are just `Navigation.getTopmost*` property lookups) to be true. When neither of those cheap conditions holds, the expensive `Map` build was wasted.

Compute the cheap navigation checks first and short-circuit before the expensive call:

```tsx
if (targetTransaction.reportID === mergeTransaction.reportID) {
    const topmostSearchReportID = Navigation.getTopmostSearchReportID();
    const isTargetThreadTopmost = !!targetTransactionThreadReportID && topmostSearchReportID === targetTransactionThreadReportID;
    const isTargetReportUnderneath = Navigation.getTopmostSuperWideRHPReportID() === targetTransaction.reportID;

    if (isTargetThreadTopmost || isTargetReportUnderneath) {
        const willTargetReportBeOneTransactionReport = willReportBecomeOneTransactionReportAfterMerge(
            targetTransaction.reportID,
            sourceTransaction.transactionID,
            targetReportTransactionsCollection,
            currentSearchResults?.data,
            isOffline,
        );

        if (!willTargetReportBeOneTransactionReport) {
            // ...existing dismissToPreviousRHP / dismissToSuperWideRHP logic...
        }
    }
}
```

This way the snapshot/collection scan only runs when the RHP underneath actually belongs to the target.

`didCurrentUserPlaceHoldOnReportExpense` is the expensive part of this block — it iterates every report action and, for each money-request action, scans `reportTransactions.find(...)` plus a `getReportAction` lookup (roughly O(actions × transactions)). It runs unconditionally here, but its result (`currentUserPlacedHold`) is only ever consumed when `hasOnlyHeldExpenses(transactions)` is true (the short-circuit `&&` on the next line). Since `getReasonAndReportActionThatRequiresAttention` is called across the LHN/report-attributes derivation for many reports, this overhead is paid on every report even when no expense is held.

Gate the expensive call behind the cheap `hasOnlyHeldExpenses(transactions)` check so it only runs when it can actually change the outcome:

```ts
const hasOnlyHeld = hasOnlyHeldExpenses(transactions);
const currentUserPlacedHold = hasOnlyHeld && didCurrentUserPlaceHoldOnReportExpense(getAllReportActions(iouReportID), transactions, currentUserAccountID);
const isExcludedForHeldExpenses = hasOnlyHeld && (actionBadge === CONST.REPORT.ACTION_BADGE.SUBMIT ? true : !currentUserPlacedHold);
```

`canPayOnPolicy` calls `canMemberWrite`, which delegates to `hasPolicyFeaturePermission` and performs object lookups (`policy?.employeeList?.[login]`, `getPolicyRole`, `ROLE_PERMISSION_BUNDLES[role]`). It is computed here at the top of the function, but its result is only used at the final `return` on line 691. The two early-return guards below (disabled reimbursement on line 680, and no designated reimburser on line 687) can short-circuit before this work is ever needed, so on those paths the permission lookup is wasted. Notably, `ReportUtils.isPayer` in this same PR already places the equivalent `canPayOnPolicy` computation *after* its early returns (line 2860) — apply the same ordering here.

Move the computation below the early-return guards:

```ts
    const isManualReimbursement = policy.reimbursementChoice === CONST.POLICY.REIMBURSEMENT_CHOICES.REIMBURSEMENT_MANUAL;

    // Reimbursement is disabled for this workspace.
    if (!isAutoReimbursement && !isManualReimbursement) {
        return false;
    }

    const reimburserEmail = policy.reimburser ?? policy.achAccount?.reimburser ?? (isManualReimbursement ? policy.owner : undefined);

    // No designated reimburser means any workspace admin can pay.
    if (!reimburserEmail) {
        return isAdmin;
    }

    const canPayOnPolicy = isAdmin || (!!currentUserLogin && canMemberWrite(policy, currentUserLogin, CONST.POLICY.POLICY_FEATURE.WORKFLOWS_PAYMENTS));
    return canPayOnPolicy && currentUserLogin === reimburserEmail;
```

`getReportOrDraftReport(parsingDetails?.reportID)` and `getPolicy(parsingDetails?.policyID || currentReport?.policyID)` were hoisted to the top of `getParsedComment` and now run unconditionally on every call. Previously each was gated behind its own guard (`if (parsingDetails?.reportID)` / `if (parsingDetails?.policyID)`). They now also execute before the `text.length > CONST.MAX_MARKUP_LENGTH` early return, so both Onyx reads are wasted when `parsingDetails` has no `reportID`/`policyID` or when the fast plaintext path is taken.

Keep the reads inside the branches that need them and short-circuit first:

```ts
function getParsedComment(text, parsingDetails, mediaAttributes, disabledRules, isGroupPolicyReportParam) {
    if (text.length > CONST.MAX_MARKUP_LENGTH) {
        return lodashEscape(text);
    }

    let isGroupPolicyReport = false;
    const currentReport = parsingDetails?.reportID ? getReportOrDraftReport(parsingDetails.reportID) : undefined;
    if (parsingDetails?.reportID) {
        if (isGroupPolicyReportParam !== undefined) {
            isGroupPolicyReport = isGroupPolicyReportParam;
        } else {
            // This will be fixed as part of https://github.com/Expensify/Expensify/issues/507850
            const policy = getPolicy(currentReport?.policyID);
            isGroupPolicyReport = isGroupPolicyByType(policy?.type);
        }
    }

    if (parsingDetails?.policyID) {
        const policy = getPolicy(parsingDetails.policyID);
        if (policy?.type) {
            isGroupPolicyReport = isGroupPolicyPolicyUtils(policy);
        }
    }
    // ...
}
```

`frozenByAdminPrefix` calls `translate('cardPage.frozenByAdminPrefix', ...)` unconditionally for every rendered card row, but the value is only ever used inside the `if (formattedFrozenDate)` block below. For the common case (a non-frozen card, where `formattedFrozenDate` is empty) this performs a wasted `translate` lookup on every row.

Move the `translate` call inside the guard so it only runs when the card is actually frozen:

```tsx
let frozenByText: string | undefined;
if (formattedFrozenDate) {
    if (item.frozenByAccountID === session?.accountID) {
        frozenByText = translate('cardPage.youFroze', {date: formattedFrozenDate});
    } else {
        const frozenByAdminPrefix = translate('cardPage.frozenByAdminPrefix', {date: formattedFrozenDate});
        frozenByText = `${frozenByAdminPrefix}${item.frozenByDisplayName ?? translate('common.someone')}`;
    }
}
```

This change moved the `getMoneyRequestParticipantsFromReport(targetReport, currentUser.accountID)` call to BEFORE the `if (!transaction) { return; }` guard. `getMoneyRequestParticipantsFromReport` does real work (resolves the chat report via `getReportOrDraftReport`, runs several `isX` checks, and iterates over participants), so it now runs even when `transaction` is falsy and the function bails out early.

Keep the expensive call after the early return:

```ts
if (!transaction) {
    return;
}
const {accountID: currentUserAccountID, email: currentUserLogin = ''} = currentUser;
const participants = getMoneyRequestParticipantsFromReport(targetReport, currentUserAccountID);
```

When `isGroupPolicyReportParam` is explicitly `true` or `false`, `getReportOrDraftReport(parsingDetails?.reportID)` on the line above is still called even though its result is not needed. This is an unnecessary Onyx read that can be avoided by checking `isGroupPolicyReportParam` before performing the lookup.

Suggested fix: restructure the conditional to skip the expensive call when the parameter is already provided:

```ts
if (parsingDetails?.reportID) {
    if (isGroupPolicyReportParam !== undefined) {
        isGroupPolicyReport = isGroupPolicyReportParam;
    } else {
        const currentReport = getReportOrDraftReport(parsingDetails?.reportID);
        isGroupPolicyReport = isReportInGroupPolicy(currentReport);
    }
}
```

`getMileageRates(policy)` is called unconditionally on every invocation of `getCustomUnitRateID`, but it is only needed when `expenseDate` is provided. When `expenseDate` is `undefined`, the condition `(!expenseDate || ...)` short-circuits immediately, making both `mileageRates` and `lastSelectedMileageRate` unused. Since `getMileageRates` iterates over policy custom units and builds a new object, this is wasted computation on the common path where no expense date is supplied.

Defer the `getMileageRates` call so it only runs when needed:

```ts
if (lastSelectedDistanceRate?.enabled && lastSelectedDistanceRateID) {
    if (expenseDate) {
        const mileageRates = getMileageRates(policy);
        const lastSelectedMileageRate = lastSelectedDistanceRateID ? mileageRates[lastSelectedDistanceRateID] : undefined;
        if (!lastSelectedMileageRate || isRateEligibleForDate(lastSelectedMileageRate, expenseDate)) {
            customUnitRateID = lastSelectedDistanceRateID;
        } else {
            const bestRate = getBestEligibleRate(mileageRates, expenseDate);
            // ... rest of date-aware logic
        }
    } else {
        customUnitRateID = lastSelectedDistanceRateID;
    }
} else if (expenseDate) {
    const mileageRates = getMileageRates(policy);
    const bestRate = getBestEligibleRate(mileageRates, expenseDate);
    // ... rest of date-aware fallback
} else {
    const defaultMileageRate = getDefaultMileageRate(policy);
    // ... rest of default fallback
}
```

The `handleSave` function is missing a `return` statement after the `updateAgentAvatar` + `Navigation.goBack` calls on lines 134-135. When `imageData.file` is truthy and `onSave` is falsy, the code calls `updateAgentAvatar` and `Navigation.goBack` but then falls through to the `if (selectedBotAvatar)` check on line 138. While these states are mutually exclusive in practice (selecting a file clears `selectedBotAvatar`), the missing `return` makes the control flow unclear and fragile.

Add a `return` after `Navigation.goBack(fallbackRoute)` on line 135:
```tsx
if (imageData.file) {
    if (onSave) {
        onSave({file: imageData.file, uri: imageData.uri});
        return;
    }
    updateAgentAvatar(accountID, {file: imageData.file, uri: imageData.uri}, personalDetails?.avatar);
    Navigation.goBack(fallbackRoute);
    return; // <-- add early return
}
```

`shouldPopoverUseScrollView(applicableSecondaryActions)` is called before the early return `if (!applicableSecondaryActions.length) { return null; }`. When the array is empty, this function call is wasted computation since the component returns `null` immediately after.

Move the call after the early return:

```tsx
if (!applicableSecondaryActions.length) {
    return null;
}

const popoverUseScrollView = shouldPopoverUseScrollView(applicableSecondaryActions);
```

The `useEffect` unconditionally creates a `setTimeout` even when `shouldDelay` is `false` (i.e., `isVisible` is already `true`). While the `setState` to the same value is a no-op in React, the timer is still allocated and scheduled by the browser on every mount, which is unnecessary work that can be avoided with a simple guard.

Add an early return inside the effect:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    if (!shouldDelay) {
        return;
    }
    const timer = setTimeout(() => setIsVisible(true), SKELETON_VISIBLE_DELAY_MS);
    return () => clearTimeout(timer);
}, [shouldDelay]);
```

This avoids creating a system timer when the skeleton should be visible immediately.

`getTransactionDetails(transaction)` and `getMergeFieldValue(...)` are hoisted to run unconditionally at the top of `getMergeFieldUpdatedValues`, but they are only used in the `field === 'amount'` and `field === 'merchant' && isDistanceRequest(transaction)` branches. `getTransactionDetails` performs non-trivial work (report lookups, amount calculations). When `field` is `'reportID'` or `'taxValue'`, these computations are wasted.

Keep `transactionDetails` and `amount` computed inside the branches that need them. For reuse across both branches, compute them lazily:

```ts
let transactionDetails: TransactionDetails | undefined;
let amount: number | undefined;

function getAmount() {
    if (amount === undefined) {
        transactionDetails ??= getTransactionDetails(transaction);
        amount = getMergeFieldValue(transactionDetails, transaction, 'amount') as number;
    }
    return amount;
}
```

Or simply duplicate the two lines in both branches since they are short.

`getOriginalTransactionWithSplitInfo` and `isPerDiemRequest` are called before checking whether `transaction` is defined (the null check happens later on line 76). When `transaction` is `undefined`, these calls are unnecessary since the condition will always be falsy. Move the `transaction` guard before the expensive work:

```ts
if (transactionIDs.length === 1) {
    const transaction = allTransactions?.[`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.TRANSACTION}${transactionIDs.at(0)}`];
    if (transaction) {
        const originalTransaction = allTransactions?.[`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.TRANSACTION}${transaction.comment?.originalTransactionID}`];
        const {isExpenseSplit} = getOriginalTransactionWithSplitInfo(transaction, originalTransaction);
        if (isExpenseSplit && isPerDiemRequest(originalTransaction ?? transaction)) {
            initSplitExpense(transaction, policy, {navigateToEditSplitExpense: true});
            return [];
        }
    }
}
```

