`Object.keys(reportActions)` allocates a temporary array of all report-action keys (which can number in the hundreds) on every call to `readNewestAction`, even when `hasOnceLoadedReportActions` is already `true` and the result is never needed. Moving the simple boolean check first avoids this unnecessary allocation in the common path.

Suggested fix -- only compute `hasReportActions` when the cheap guard fails:

```ts
if (!hasOnceLoadedReportActions) {
    const reportActions = allReportActions?.[reportID];
    const hasReportActions = !!reportActions && Object.keys(reportActions).length > 0;
    if (!hasReportActions) {
        return;
    }
}
