The old code had an early return (`if (!shouldHandleNavigation) { return; }`) after `mergeTransactionIdsHighlightOnSearchRoute`, which avoided computing navigation state when navigation was suppressed. The refactored code removes this early return, so when `navigationHandler` is `() => {}` (the no-op case), the function still computes `navigationRef.getRootState()`, `isSearchTopmostFullScreenRoute()`, `getCurrentSearchQueryJSON()`, and derived values like `alreadyOnSearchRoot` and `isSameSearchType` -- all before passing them into a callback that will never execute them.

These navigation state lookups are not free and run unnecessarily when navigation is suppressed. The telemetry calls (`setPendingSubmitFollowUpAction`, `startSpan`) are correctly inside the callback, but the state computations on lines 1199-1205 are outside it.

Move the navigation state computations (lines 1199-1205) inside the `executeNavigation` callback so they are skipped when the handler is a no-op:

```typescript
executeNavigation(() => {
    const rootState = navigationRef.getRootState();
    const searchNavigatorRoute = rootState?.routes?.findLast(...);
    const lastSearchRoute = searchNavigatorRoute?.state?.routes?.at(-1);
    const alreadyOnSearchRoot = isSearchTopmostFullScreenRoute() && lastSearchRoute?.name === SCREENS.SEARCH.ROOT;
    const currentSearchQueryJSON = alreadyOnSearchRoot ? getCurrentSearchQueryJSON() : undefined;
    const isSameSearchType = currentSearchQueryJSON?.type === type;
    setPendingSubmitFollowUpAction(...);
    startSpan(...);
    // ... navigation logic
});
```

